-- belief.lth: a Gaussian belief about a parameter in information form, the maximizer Laplace's
-- update needs, stated as a theory so that the host's can be validated, and the update itself.
--
-- Draft 0, 2026-10-14, by Claude Fable 5.1, an agent of the coordinating session, on the brief
-- record/IA/slice-theories-2-2026-10-14/prompt.md (item 2; inventory §6 item 7, row 22; the
-- callback crossing of D-2026-10-14-a). Sources: spec 7.R2 (over `VecOf n`), 3.S23 as it stands
-- (a Luthier lambda passed to the host is a callback; a record whose fields are functions
-- crosses as the record), 10.S8 to 10.S10 (an extern is a route, validated against its type's
-- laws at the first action that asks for it), 11.S18 (a tolerance is the demand's), 7.R4 (a law
-- over the models of a theory is certified per model), 4.R10, P11 (every criterion is a
-- parameter: the maximizer's tolerance, budget and floor). The v0.1 file is `belief.lut` at the
-- tag v0.1-final (lines 110-136 are what the slice uses); the names are its. What is not here:
-- v0.1's `BeliefFamily`, `Discrete`, the evidence and the calibration laws, which need a
-- sampler and the generator of normals probability.lth lacks; and `laplaceEvidence`, which
-- needs a log-determinant no leaf gives.
-- 2026-10-15, by Claude Opus 5.5, an agent of the coordinating session, on record/IA/
-- layer-3-review-2026-10-15/prompt-fix-BCD.md (items 1 and 4 of its summary-BCD.md, and the
-- runner-up on `Posterior`): the score of a posterior a gradient along a drawn direction; its
-- information not negative, so that optimization.lth's `negate` is total; `QuadraticConverges`
-- over the settings whose budget is at least the demand's budget for the family, the law that
-- refutes a maximizer that gives up everywhere.
-- 2026-10-26, by Claude Opus 5.5 (night Agent V), on record/plan/after-core-2026-10-23/
-- prompt-night-2026-10-26.md and D-2026-10-16-m: a maximizer says why it stopped (`StopReason`,
-- moved here from optimization.lth) and after how many iterations (`MaxResult`'s `steps`), and
-- declares its bound on the quadratic family (`quadraticSteps`); `MaximizerStationary` checks every
-- reason, no `else true`; `QuadraticConverges` owes convergence on a domain drawn from the problem
-- (`quadraticReachable`) within the maximizer's bound, no demand's `budget`.
--
-- What this file states. A Gaussian belief on the vectors of dimension n is a mean and a
-- precision, the inverse covariance; zero precision along a direction is a belief that knows
-- nothing there, how the gauge of the positions enters without inventing a scale. Its laws: the
-- precision is symmetric and its log density is greatest at the mean (the precision is not
-- negative on any direction). A maximizer is a theory of one function: given the settings, the
-- objective with its score and its information on the vectors of a dimension and a start, it
-- gives where it stopped, why and after how many steps (v0.1's `Maximizer` was a bare function
-- type with its laws in optimization.lut; here the laws are the type's, so that 10.S10 can
-- validate the host's `lm.maximize`, belief_model.lut, at its first action). The laws: the
-- objective does not fall from the start; the reason it gives is the one the Newton decrement,
-- half of g^T M^+ g at the settings' floor, and the budget show; the dimension is kept; and on a
-- concave quadratic, whose maximum is known, it converges within the steps its theory bounds. The first three are stated
-- over any `Posterior`, a record of functions with no generator, so they are certified per
-- problem (7.R4, listed cases); the quadratic family has the generators of `VecOf n` and
-- `Positive`, so it is sampled. Laplace's update is then a definition on the parts: the new mean
-- is the maximizer's `x` on belief times likelihood, the new precision the prior's plus the
-- information there; its laws are those of its parts (L9.2) and none is restated.
--
-- Index (all sections written in this draft):
--   §1 `GaussianBelief`, `gaussianLogDensity`, the laws of a belief (v0.1 belief.lut:110-116). done
--   §2 `Settings`, `StopReason`, `MaxResult`, `Posterior`, `Maximizer` and the laws a maximizer must meet
--      (v0.1 belief.lut:118-122, optimization.lut:118-128).                                   done
--   §3 Laplace's update: `laplacePosterior`, `laplaceMode`, `laplaceAt`, `laplaceUpdate`
--      (v0.1 belief.lut:124-136).                                                              done

import "numbers.lth" Tolerance Positive within withinOf abs Doubles
import "arrays.lth" Vec Mat VecOf MatOf Dim vec mat identity approxVec approxMat exact RankFloor
import "arrays_model.lut" numpy : Arrays add sub scale dot norm matVec matMul madd mscale transpose pinvSym
import "probability.lth" Likelihood StepResolution IsGradient

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §1 Gaussian beliefs

-- Information form on the vectors of dimension n: the mean and the precision, n x n. The log
-- density, up to its constant (v0.1 belief.lut:113-116).
GaussianBelief (n : Nat) : { mean : VecOf n, precision : MatOf n n }
gaussianLogDensity (n : Nat) (b : GaussianBelief n) (t : VecOf n) : Float64 :=
  { d := sub t b.mean
    0.0 - 0.5 * dot d (matVec b.precision d) }

-- the precision is symmetric within the tolerance, and the log density is greatest at the mean:
-- zero there, and elsewhere minus half a quadratic form that is not negative beyond rounding
-- (the precision is positive semidefinite, read through the density, which is what the belief
-- says; a zero direction is allowed, the belief knows nothing there)
-- spec 11.S18
PrecisionSymmetric (n : Nat) (b : GaussianBelief n) (tol : Tolerance) : Bool := approxMat tol b.precision (transpose b.precision)
DensityPeaksAtMean (n : Nat) (b : GaussianBelief n) (tol : Tolerance) (t : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  { q := gaussianLogDensity n b t
    gaussianLogDensity n b b.mean == 0.0 and (q <= 0.0 or within tol q 0.0) }
GaussianBelief (n : Nat) := PrecisionSymmetric n & DensityPeaksAtMean n

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §2 The maximizer (3.S23, 10.S10, P11)

-- The settings a maximizer runs with, the demand's (P11; experiment_zero.lut:98 took `tol` and
-- `maxIter` from its parameters): the tolerance of the decrement at which it has converged, the
-- budget of iterations, and the floor of the step matrix's spectrum below which a direction is
-- not stepped nor counted in the decrement. [proposed] The floor is the third setting (v0.1's
-- `solve` inverted the matrix whole, and raised where it was singular).
Settings : U := { tol : Positive, maxIter : Nat, floor : RankFloor | 0.0 < tol and 0 < maxIter and 0.0 <= floor }

-- why a maximizer stopped (D-2026-10-16-m, BE2): converged, the decrement within the settings'
-- tolerance; exhausted, the budget of iterations spent first; stalled, the method could make no
-- more progress before either (for Levenberg-Marquardt, the damping beyond its most). A run that
-- has not stopped has no reason, so `running` is no alternative here (optimization.lth's `finished`
-- gives `Maybe StopReason`). Moved here from optimization.lth on 2026-10-26, so that `MaxResult`
-- carries it.
StopReason = { converged : Unit || exhausted : Unit || stalled : Unit }
isConverged (r : StopReason) : Bool := match r { converged u -> true ; _ -> false }

-- where a maximizer stopped on the vectors of dimension n, why, and after how many iterations
-- (D-2026-10-16-m): the step count is the algorithm's resource, recorded for the planner (L6.3)
-- and the first alarm of a run taking far more steps than its theory says. [2026-10-26] It was
-- `{ x, converged }`; `isConverged r.reason` is the old field.
MaxResult (n : Nat) = { x : VecOf n, reason : StopReason, steps : Nat }

-- What a maximizer is given: a log posterior with its score and its information on the vectors
-- of dimension n, as three functions (what crosses to the host, each a callback, D-2026-10-14-a).
-- The laws: the score is the gradient of the log posterior at the demand's resolution, along a
-- drawn direction; the information is symmetric and its quadratic form on a drawn direction is
-- not negative beyond rounding, as a likelihood's is (probability.lth `InfoNonNegative`).
-- [proposed] The last law is new: without it `info := -I` is a posterior and not the step matrix
-- of an objective, so optimization.lth's `negate` was not total (summary-BCD.md, runner-up).
-- Certified per posterior (7.R4).
Posterior (n : Nat) : { logp : VecOf n -> Float64, score : VecOf n -> Vec Float64, info : VecOf n -> Mat Float64 }
PosteriorScoreIsGradient (n : Nat) (p : Posterior n) (r : StepResolution) (t v : VecOf n) : Bool := IsGradient n r p.logp p.score t v
PosteriorInfoSymmetric (n : Nat) (p : Posterior n) (tol : Tolerance) (t : VecOf n) : Bool := approxMat tol (p.info t) (transpose (p.info t))
PosteriorInfoNonNegative (n : Nat) (p : Posterior n) (tol : Tolerance) (t v : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  { q := dot v (matVec (p.info t) v)
    0.0 <= q or within tol q 0.0 }
Posterior (n : Nat) := PosteriorScoreIsGradient n & PosteriorInfoSymmetric n & PosteriorInfoNonNegative n

-- the Newton decrement of a log posterior at x: half of g^T M^+ g, the increase the information
-- predicts to the maximum, with the pseudo-inverse at the settings' floor (v0.1
-- optimization.lut:51 solved the matrix whole)
decrement (n : Nat) (s : Settings) (g : VecOf n -> Vec Float64) (m : VecOf n -> Mat Float64) (x : VecOf n) : Float64 :=
  { gx := g x
    0.5 * dot gx (matVec (pinvSym s.floor (m x)) gx) }

-- A maximizer: the settings, the dimension, the log posterior, its score, its information and a
-- start in; where it stopped, why and after how many iterations out. It evaluates the functions
-- only at vectors of that dimension, which their types say (v0.1 belief.lut:122). With it, its
-- theory of cost on the one family whose maximum is known (D-2026-10-16-m, D-2026-10-18-d: the laws
-- of an algorithm say how its budget scales): `quadraticSteps s dec`, the iterations within which
-- it stops converged on a concave quadratic whose decrement at the start is `dec`, under settings
-- `s`. It is a function of the problem derived from the method's theory, never a constant (P11):
-- Newton's method 1 (0 when the start is within the tolerance); Levenberg-Marquardt a handful
-- (optimization.lth `lmQuadraticSteps`); gradient ascent about kappa log(dec / tol), kappa the
-- condition number, 1 on this family. [proposed] The bound is a field of the model, so that each
-- method states its own (2026-10-26, Agent V).
Maximizer : {
  maximize : Settings -> (n : Nat) -> (VecOf n -> Float64) -> (VecOf n -> Vec Float64) -> (VecOf n -> Mat Float64) -> VecOf n -> MaxResult n,
  quadraticSteps : Settings -> Float64 -> Nat,
}

-- what any maximizer must meet, on any posterior (v0.1 optimization.lut:118-128): the log
-- posterior does not fall from the start; the reason it gives is the one the posterior shows,
-- recomputed from the posterior and not from the run: converged, the decrement there within the
-- settings' tolerance; exhausted, the budget spent and the decrement not within the tolerance;
-- stalled, the budget not spent and the decrement not within it; and never more iterations than
-- the budget; and the dimension is kept. Whether it converges is a quality of the method on a
-- problem, not a law, except on the domain where its theory says it must: a concave quadratic,
-- below. [2026-10-26, D-2026-10-16-m] `MaximizerStationary` was `if r.converged then decrement
-- <= tol else true`: a maximizer that gave up everywhere passed it vacuously. Now every reason is
-- checked, and no case is vacuous. The strict `<` of not converging is `finished`'s test.
-- spec 10.S10, P11
MaximizerAscends (mx : Maximizer) (s : Settings) (n : Dim) (p : Posterior n) (x0 : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  p.logp (mx.maximize s n p.logp p.score p.info x0).x >= p.logp x0
MaximizerStationary (mx : Maximizer) (s : Settings) (n : Dim) (p : Posterior n) (x0 : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  { r := mx.maximize s n p.logp p.score p.info x0
    dec := decrement n s p.score p.info r.x
    r.steps <= s.maxIter
    and (match r.reason {
           converged u -> dec <= s.tol ;
           exhausted u -> r.steps == s.maxIter and not (dec < s.tol) ;
           stalled u -> r.steps < s.maxIter and not (dec < s.tol) }) }
MaximizerKeepsDimension (mx : Maximizer) (s : Settings) (n : Dim) (p : Posterior n) (x0 : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  (mx.maximize s n p.logp p.score p.info x0).x.n == n

-- the concave quadratic with its maximum at b and curvature c on every direction: the family
-- with generators (`VecOf n`, `Positive`), the domain where a maximizer's convergence is a law
-- (D-2026-10-16-m). Its decrement at x is c |x - b|^2 / 2, so ending at b within the decrement's
-- tolerance is c |x - b|^2 / 2 <= tol.
quadratic (n : Nat) (b : VecOf n) (c : Positive) : Posterior n := {
  logp := fn x { d := sub x b
                 0.0 - 0.5 * c * dot d d },
  score := fn x { scale (0.0 - c) (sub x b) },
  info := fn x { mscale c (identity n) },
}
-- [proposed, 2026-10-26] The quadratics on which convergence is owed: those whose decrement, at
-- the points within one unit in the last place of b in every coordinate, is below the tolerance.
-- A run in floating point ends at best there (the unit at b_i is at most epsilon |b_i|, numbers.lth
-- `Doubles`), so where c epsilon^2 |b|^2 / 2 reaches the tolerance not converging is the right
-- answer. The bound is the format's, not a criterion (P11).
quadraticReachable (fp : Doubles Float64) (s : Settings) (n : Nat) (b : VecOf n) (c : Positive) : Bool :=
  0.5 * c * (fp.epsilon * fp.epsilon) * dot b b < s.tol
-- On that domain a maximizer stops within its own bound, `mx.quadraticSteps` at the decrement of
-- the start, and either converges at b within the tolerance, or the settings' budget is below the
-- bound and it stopped exhausted at the budget. Not converging within the bound is a failure, not
-- a vacuous case (D-2026-10-16-m); a stall is a failure. Outside the domain the law asks nothing:
-- the premise is on the problem, drawn, not on what the maximizer says, so the tester's count of
-- the cases where it held (report-wave-a-3.md, A3) says how much the law was asked. [2026-10-26]
-- It was `if s.maxIter < budget then true else (converged and at b)`, `budget` a census of the
-- demand (50): a constant, and vacuous at every budget below it.
QuadraticConverges (mx : Maximizer) (fp : Doubles Float64) (s : Settings) (n : Dim) (b : VecOf n) (c : Positive) (x0 : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  { q := quadratic n b c
    if quadraticReachable fp s n b c
    then {
      bound := mx.quadraticSteps s (decrement n s q.score q.info x0)
      r := mx.maximize s n q.logp q.score q.info x0
      d := sub r.x b
      r.steps <= bound
      and (match r.reason {
             converged u -> 0.5 * c * dot d d <= s.tol ;
             exhausted u -> s.maxIter < bound and r.steps == s.maxIter ;
             stalled u -> false }) }
    else true }
Maximizer := MaximizerAscends & MaximizerStationary & MaximizerKeepsDimension & QuadraticConverges
-- [proposed] Two maximizers end at the same point of the quadratic family, within the tolerance,
-- and say the same about convergence: no law of the theory, the question of
-- record/IA/evidence-2026-10-14/prompt.md, item 3. Moved 2026-10-16 from tests/tester/fit_laws.test.lut
-- so that its entries have a file that claims them (L2.8, L10.12).
MaximizersAgree (mx my : Maximizer) (tol : Tolerance) (s : Settings) (n : Dim) (b : VecOf n) (c : Positive) (x0 : VecOf n) : Bool :=
  { q := quadratic n b c
    r1 := mx.maximize s n q.logp q.score q.info x0
    r2 := my.maximize s n q.logp q.score q.info x0
    isConverged r1.reason == isConverged r2.reason and approxVec tol r1.x r2.x }

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------- §3 Laplace's update

-- belief times likelihood on datum y, as the posterior a maximizer is given: the log posterior
-- up to its constant, its score and its information, the prior's precision plus the
-- likelihood's (v0.1 belief.lut:127-131, the three lambdas of laplaceMode)
laplacePosterior (n : Nat) (l : Likelihood n) (b : GaussianBelief n) (y : Vec Float64) : Posterior n := {
  logp := fn t { gaussianLogDensity n b t + l.loglik y t },
  score := fn t { sub (l.score y t) (matVec b.precision (sub t b.mean)) },
  info := fn t { madd b.precision (l.info y t) },
}

-- Laplace's update: the new mean is the maximum of belief times likelihood, found from the
-- prior mean; the new precision the prior's plus the information the likelihood reports there
laplaceMode (n : Nat) (mx : Maximizer) (s : Settings) (l : Likelihood n) (b : GaussianBelief n) (y : Vec Float64) : MaxResult n :=
  { p := laplacePosterior n l b y
    mx.maximize s n p.logp p.score p.info b.mean }
-- the Gaussian belief at a mode m: mean m, precision the prior's plus the information there
laplaceAt (n : Nat) (l : Likelihood n) (b : GaussianBelief n) (y : Vec Float64) (m : VecOf n) : GaussianBelief n :=
  { mean := m, precision := madd b.precision (l.info y m) }
laplaceUpdate (n : Nat) (mx : Maximizer) (s : Settings) (l : Likelihood n) (b : GaussianBelief n) (y : Vec Float64) : GaussianBelief n :=
  laplaceAt n l b y (laplaceMode n mx s l b y).x
-- [core bite 12] gathered so that this file claims it (4.S14), as arrays.lth gathers
-- `ArrayContainers`: not a law of `Maximizer`, a comparison of two
MaximizerComparisons := MaximizersAgree
